.TH BEARM 1 "July 2026" "Bearm" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bearm \- remove safely and restore confidently
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bearm rm
.RI [ OPTIONS ] " TARGET" ...
.br
.B bearm list
.RI [ \-\-limit " N" ]
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B bearm restore
.IR ITEM_ID ...
.br
.B bearm restore
.BR \-\-operation " " OPERATION_ID
.br
.B bearm restore \-\-last
.br
.B bearm purge
.IR ITEM_ID ...
.RI [ \-\-yes ]
.br
.B bearm doctor
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B bearm config
.BR path | check
.br
.B bearm version
.SH DESCRIPTION
Bearm is a native safe replacement for Unix
.BR rm (1).
Compatibility-mode removal moves files, symlinks, and directories into an
operating-system trash location instead of permanently deleting them.
Native commands list, restore, purge, and diagnose Bearm-managed trash items.
.PP
Bearm uses direct filesystem operations, performs no runtime network access,
and sends no telemetry.
.SH COMPATIBILITY MODE
Compatibility mode is selected by the
.B rm
subcommand or when the executable basename is
.BR rm .
Linux selects the GNU profile and macOS selects the BSD profile by default.
.B BEARM_COMPAT
may select
.BR gnu ,
.BR bsd ,
or
.BR posix .
.PP
GNU parsing accepts options after operands until
.BR \-\- .
BSD and POSIX parsing stop at the first operand.
Force and interaction options are applied from left to right.
Successful operations are silent unless verbose mode is active.
.SH NATIVE COMMANDS
.TP
.B list
List active trash items. Use
.B \-\-limit
to bound text or JSON results and
.B \-\-json
for machine-readable records.
.TP
.B restore
Restore item IDs,
.B \-\-operation
to restore an operation, or
.B \-\-last
to restore the latest active operation.
.TP
.B purge
Permanently delete selected active items. Purge requires an interactive
confirmation or
.BR \-\-yes .
.TP
.B doctor
Report incomplete journal data or missing active trash items. Use
.B \-\-json
for machine-readable findings.
.TP
.B config path
Print the active TOML configuration path.
.TP
.B config check
Validate the active configuration.
.TP
.B version
Print Bearm version, commit, and build date.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Ignore nonexistent targets and disable earlier interactive behavior.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive=always
Prompt before each logical target.
.TP
.BR \-I ", " \-\-interactive=once
Prompt once for recursive or large requests.
.TP
.B \-\-interactive=never
Disable prompting in the GNU profile.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-R ", " \-\-recursive
Permit directory removal.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dir ", " \-\-directory
Permit removal of empty directories without recursive mode.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print each successful logical removal.
.TP
.B \-\-one\-file\-system
Do not cross mount boundaries when GNU-profile traversal is required.
.TP
.BR \-\-preserve\-root [ =all ]
Protect the system root, or protect directory operands on a device different
from their parent.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-preserve\-root
Disable GNU compatibility root preservation. Bearm hard protection for
.B /
remains active.
.TP
.B \-\-
End option parsing.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Bearm loads defaults, strict TOML configuration, then environment overrides.
Unknown TOML fields and invalid values are rejected before filesystem
mutation.
.PP
The complete version 1 schema and precedence rules are documented in
.IR docs/configuration.md .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BEARM_LANG
Native language:
.B pt-BR
or
.BR en .
.TP
.B BEARM_COMPAT
Compatibility profile:
.BR gnu ,
.BR bsd ,
or
.BR posix .
.TP
.B BEARM_TRASH
Absolute custom trash root.
.TP
.B BEARM_TRASH_PER_MOUNT
Boolean Linux per-mount routing override.
.TP
.B BEARM_LOG
Local diagnostic level:
.B error
or
.BR debug .
.TP
.BR BEARM_CONFIG_HOME ", " BEARM_STATE_HOME ", " BEARM_DATA_HOME
Absolute Bearm directory overrides.
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_STATE_HOME ", " XDG_DATA_HOME
Linux base-directory overrides.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_MESSAGES ", " LANG
Compatibility diagnostic locale precedence.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/bearm/config.toml
Linux configuration, falling back to
.IR $HOME/.config/bearm/config.toml .
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/bearm/journal.jsonl
Linux journal, falling back to
.IR $HOME/.local/state/bearm/journal.jsonl .
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash
Linux home trash, falling back to
.IR $HOME/.local/share/Trash .
.TP
.I $HOME/Library/Application Support/Bearm/config.toml
macOS configuration.
.TP
.I $HOME/Library/Application Support/Bearm/state/journal.jsonl
macOS journal.
.TP
.I $HOME/.Trash
macOS home-volume Trash.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Compatibility mode returns 0 for success, 1 for GNU usage or operational
failure, and 64 for BSD usage failure.
.PP
Native mode returns 0 for success, 1 for an operational failure, 2 for invalid
usage, and 3 for invalid configuration or directory overrides.
.SH SAFETY
Bearm rejects the system root, dot and dot-dot operands, its configuration,
state, data, and resolved trash roots. Final symlink operands are moved as
symlinks and their destinations are not followed.
.PP
Destination names and metadata are reserved with exclusive creation. A custom
trash move that crosses filesystems fails without copying or deleting the
source. Only native
.B purge
permanently deletes Bearm-managed trash items.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Windows is not supported. Finder Put Back metadata is not guaranteed.
Cross-filesystem copying into custom trash is unsupported. BSD
.B \-P
and
.B \-W
semantics are not implemented. Trash is not a backup.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Move a file to trash:
.PP
.EX
bearm rm report.txt
.EE
.PP
Move a directory without ordinary descendant traversal:
.PP
.EX
bearm rm -rf build
.EE
.PP
Restore the latest active operation:
.PP
.EX
bearm restore --last
.EE
.PP
Create a reversible shell alias:
.PP
.EX
alias rm='bearm rm'
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rm (1),
.BR mv (1)
